# Line-ending policy.
#
# Kiln is developed on Windows (where Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default) but
# runs on Linux and macOS too. Without this file, whether a contributor commits LF or CRLF
# depends entirely on their local autocrlf setting — and a CRLF-terminated shell script fails
# on Linux with the famously unhelpful `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`.

# Let git decide for anything not named below, normalising to LF in the repository.
* text=auto

# The POSIX shims are executed by /usr/bin/env; they MUST have LF in the working tree, on
# every platform, regardless of the checking-out developer's autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell keeps the platform convention it is written for.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary

logbook.md merge=union
